1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,279
One question decides more points in Gate
2 than any other: "what does the server

2
00:00:09,319 --> 00:00:14,098
have?" There are two ways to answer it.
One reads a ref, a name such as a branch

3
00:00:14,138 --> 00:00:18,375
that holds a commit ID, in your own
clone, your copy of the repository.

4
00:00:18,415 --> 00:00:21,869
The other asks the server, the
repository the team shares.

5
00:00:21,909 --> 00:00:26,869
Today you watch both answers, one wrong and
one right, and you fix the habit before

6
00:00:26,909 --> 00:00:30,784
the gate tests it. Which is your
habit today? The demo checks it.

7
00:00:32,624 --> 00:00:36,327
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

8
00:00:36,367 --> 00:00:41,359
This is a gate briefing, not a lesson. A
gate is the examination that ends a block

9
00:00:41,399 --> 00:00:46,513
of the course. The briefing explains what
Gate 2 covers, how it's scored, and how to

10
00:00:46,553 --> 00:00:50,993
prepare. It shows no gate item, and
the gate file isn't opened on screen.

11
00:00:51,033 --> 00:00:56,147
The rule from the assessments file applies:
a gate that has been read is a gate that

12
00:00:56,187 --> 00:00:57,068
has been taken.

13
00:00:57,408 --> 00:01:02,687
You take Gate 2 after Module 7. If you've
done the labs of Modules 6 and 7 by hand,

14
00:01:02,727 --> 00:01:05,507
you've already practised
everything it asks.

15
00:01:07,347 --> 00:01:08,799
After this briefing you can:

16
00:01:09,139 --> 00:01:12,364
State what Gate 2 covers
and its threshold of 90.

17
00:01:12,704 --> 00:01:15,909
Explain why the threshold
is higher than for Gate 1.

18
00:01:16,249 --> 00:01:20,606
Prepare the prediction part by drawing
graphs with local and remote-tracking refs.

19
00:01:20,946 --> 00:01:35,129
Run the hands-on part with the ritual
first and the server asked directly.

20
00:01:36,969 --> 00:01:40,909
Gate 2, Branching. Pass at
90. Taken after Module 7.

21
00:01:40,949 --> 00:01:47,340
It covers branches as refs, divergence,
ancestry, upstream tracking, remote-tracking

22
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:49,407
refs, fetch, pull and push.

23
00:01:49,747 --> 00:01:53,927
The structure is the same as Gate
1: 100 points in four parts.

24
00:01:53,967 --> 00:01:59,235
Concepts, 30 points: six written questions,
closed book, no terminal, and each

25
00:01:59,275 --> 00:02:04,951
answer needs a mechanism. Prediction, 20
points: four items, no terminal, and object

26
00:02:04,991 --> 00:02:10,532
IDs aren't asked for. Hands-on diagnosis,
30 points, in a repository that a script

27
00:02:10,572 --> 00:02:15,636
builds in a broken state, with a report
that's incomplete and partly wrong.

28
00:02:15,676 --> 00:02:19,992
Oral interview, 20 points: six
questions, each with a follow-up.

29
00:02:20,332 --> 00:02:26,183
The pass rule: the threshold overall,
and at least 70 percent in every part.

30
00:02:26,223 --> 00:02:32,462
That is 21 of 30, 14 of 20, 21 of 30 and
14 of 20. A total above 90 with one part

31
00:02:32,502 --> 00:02:34,555
below 70 percent is a miss.

32
00:02:34,895 --> 00:02:39,900
Why 90 here, and 85 for Gate 1? The
assessments file gives the numbers, and the

33
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:44,817
roadmap fixed them. Think of it this
way. Up to Gate 1, every command you ran

34
00:02:44,857 --> 00:02:50,181
affected one repository, yours. From this
block on, commands reach a repository that

35
00:02:50,221 --> 00:02:53,821
other people share, and an
error with push isn't private.

36
00:02:53,861 --> 00:02:58,930
So the gate asks for more, and in the
hands-on part it scores the safety of your

37
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:00,782
path, not only the end state.

38
00:03:01,122 --> 00:03:06,437
The hands-on points are split three ways.
The end state, which check.sh inspects.

39
00:03:06,477 --> 00:03:09,810
The safety of the path,
read from your command log.

40
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:14,901
And the explanation, written as root-cause
boxes. The file says it plainly: a

41
00:03:14,941 --> 00:03:20,389
correct end state reached through git reset
--hard, a forced push or a re-clone can

42
00:03:20,429 --> 00:03:22,108
still fail the safety row.

43
00:03:22,448 --> 00:03:25,457
And the two typical ways to
lose points in this gate.

44
00:03:25,497 --> 00:03:30,233
First, trusting a remote-tracking ref, your
clone's record of where a server branch

45
00:03:30,273 --> 00:03:35,066
was, as if it were the server. Second,
repairing with force where an integration was

46
00:03:35,106 --> 00:03:35,584
possible.

47
00:03:37,424 --> 00:03:42,468
One sentence to take into the room. For
every ref in a problem, say which repository

48
00:03:42,508 --> 00:03:44,949
it lives in before you say anything else.

49
00:03:45,289 --> 00:03:49,036
main in your clone, origin/main in your
clone, main on the server, main in a

50
00:03:49,076 --> 00:03:53,221
teammate's clone. Four refs, three repositories,
and two of the four are in the same

51
00:03:53,261 --> 00:03:53,470
.git.

52
00:03:53,810 --> 00:03:57,081
origin/main is a dated
photograph of the server's main.

53
00:03:57,121 --> 00:04:02,079
It was accurate when it was taken. Now a
teammate pushes, and in your clone nothing

54
00:04:02,119 --> 00:04:07,076
moves. The photograph doesn't update itself,
and looking at it harder doesn't help.

55
00:04:07,416 --> 00:04:10,971
For ancestry questions, use
section 7.8 of the textbook.

56
00:04:11,011 --> 00:04:15,592
Two branches have diverged when neither
tip is an ancestor of the other.

57
00:04:15,632 --> 00:04:20,855
Ahead and behind are counted from the merge
base. And A..B is everything reachable

58
00:04:20,895 --> 00:04:26,118
from B but not from A. State the base every
time: "3 commits ahead" is meaningless

59
00:04:26,158 --> 00:04:27,915
until you say ahead of what.

60
00:04:29,755 --> 00:04:32,817
In the prediction part, draw
this before you answer.

61
00:04:32,857 --> 00:04:37,768
Put every ref of the item into one of the
boxes. Most wrong predictions come from a

62
00:04:37,808 --> 00:04:39,379
ref drawn in the wrong box.

63
00:04:39,719 --> 00:04:44,993
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Draw the three boxes and place those four

64
00:04:45,033 --> 00:04:47,885
refs. Which box holds
two? Say it out loud.

65
00:04:52,725 --> 00:04:57,595
Your clone: main and origin/main. The name
tempts you to draw origin/main on the

66
00:04:57,635 --> 00:04:59,497
server. It lives in your clone.

67
00:05:01,337 --> 00:05:04,153
The question is "what does the
server have?" First answer:

68
00:05:04,493 --> 00:05:10,236
"Up to date with 'origin/main'." Both
refs at 510ee94, both in this clone.

69
00:05:10,276 --> 00:05:16,489
This answered a different question. Now
predict: is the server's main at 510ee94

70
00:05:16,529 --> 00:05:18,990
too? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

71
00:05:23,830 --> 00:05:24,814
Second answer:

72
00:05:25,154 --> 00:05:30,180
The server's main begins with ef22149.
That's the answer to the question that was

73
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:33,370
asked, and asking the server
is the habit to build.

74
00:05:33,710 --> 00:05:38,520
Quick quiz. git fetch is next.
Which refs in your clone move?

75
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:42,655
A, your main. B, only
remote-tracking refs. C, both.

76
00:05:42,695 --> 00:05:43,609
Your answer?

77
00:05:48,449 --> 00:05:51,493
B. git fetch 🟢 SAFE moves
only remote-tracking refs.

78
00:05:51,533 --> 00:05:53,628
Watch it, and then the status again.

79
00:05:59,168 --> 00:06:03,685
Behind by one commit. Nothing of yours
moved, and now the local view is true.

80
00:06:04,025 --> 00:06:07,981
As a picture: one label moved,
origin/main. Your main didn't.

81
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:13,882
For the hands-on part, run the generator
of variant A, which prints the sandbox

82
00:06:13,922 --> 00:06:17,639
path. Open the lab shell
there, and read SYMPTOMS.md.

83
00:06:17,679 --> 00:06:22,176
Don't read generate.sh or check.sh.
Keep a log of every command.

84
00:06:22,516 --> 00:06:27,017
Start with the diagnosis ritual
from Chapter 1, as in Gate 1.

85
00:06:27,057 --> 00:06:33,271
Then add two commands from this block before
you change anything: git branch -vv for

86
00:06:33,311 --> 00:06:38,110
every branch's upstream, and git
ls-remote for the server itself.

87
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:44,364
And git log --oneline --graph --decorate
--all gives you the graph with every ref on

88
00:06:44,404 --> 00:06:44,587
it.

89
00:06:46,427 --> 00:06:48,075
Five mistakes to watch for.

90
00:06:48,415 --> 00:06:51,528
Answering "what does the
server have" from git status.

91
00:06:51,568 --> 00:06:55,441
Root cause: status compares two
local refs and opens no connection.

92
00:06:55,781 --> 00:07:00,953
Fixing a rejected push with --force. Root
cause: rejected meant the server's tip was

93
00:07:00,993 --> 00:07:06,166
not an ancestor of yours; an integration
was possible, and the safety of the path is

94
00:07:06,206 --> 00:07:06,600
scored.

95
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:11,786
Changing state before collecting evidence.
Root cause: the command log is read for

96
00:07:11,826 --> 00:07:15,243
evidence before change and a
way back before each rewrite.

97
00:07:15,583 --> 00:07:21,914
Scoring 92 overall with 13 in Prediction.
Root cause: the pass rule needs 70% in

98
00:07:21,954 --> 00:07:22,790
every part.

99
00:07:23,130 --> 00:07:27,773
Opening the gate file "to see what is in
it". Root cause: a gate that has been read

100
00:07:27,813 --> 00:07:29,466
is a gate that has been taken.

101
00:07:31,306 --> 00:07:36,415
Now, out of the lab. The habit this gate
checks is the release engineer's habit from

102
00:07:36,455 --> 00:07:41,196
video 39: a release script that begins
with git fetch, and refuses to continue

103
00:07:41,236 --> 00:07:45,915
unless the commit it's about to tag
equals what git ls-remote reports for the

104
00:07:45,955 --> 00:07:50,819
server's main. And the engineer's habit
from video 41: reading whether a refusal

105
00:07:50,859 --> 00:07:54,190
says rejected or remote
rejected before doing anything.

106
00:07:54,230 --> 00:07:58,726
If you would act that way on a release
day, you'll act that way in Part 3.

107
00:08:00,566 --> 00:08:05,060
Your turn. Redo three exercises
without notes, all in

108
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:07,883
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md:

109
00:08:08,223 --> 00:08:13,279
Exercise 6.9, Level 4, "a merge
that somebody else left half done".

110
00:08:13,619 --> 00:08:19,259
Exercise 7.9, Level 4, "a clone that has
not talked to the server for a week".

111
00:08:19,599 --> 00:08:25,694
Exercise 7.10, Level 5, "the hotfix that
was pushed and is not on the server".

112
00:08:26,034 --> 00:08:30,500
For each, before the first command, draw
the three boxes and place every ref.

113
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:34,713
Then write your prediction for the
first two commands you intend to run.

114
00:08:35,053 --> 00:08:38,762
When all three go through
without notes, take Gate 2:

115
00:08:38,802 --> 00:08:43,076
assessments/gate-2-branching.md.
One sitting, parts in order.

116
00:08:44,916 --> 00:08:49,983
Q92: "What is the difference between HEAD,
a branch and a remote-tracking branch?"

117
00:08:52,323 --> 00:08:57,352
Answer out loud. A strong answer says, for
each of the three, what it is in storage,

118
00:08:57,392 --> 00:09:02,299
in which repository it lives, and what
moves it. It doesn't use the word "pointer"

119
00:09:02,339 --> 00:09:07,367
for all three and stop. The follow-up in an
oral part will probe exactly the one you

120
00:09:07,407 --> 00:09:08,755
described most vaguely.

121
00:09:10,595 --> 00:09:13,522
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

122
00:09:13,862 --> 00:09:18,452
Gate 2 covers branches as refs,
divergence, ancestry, upstream tracking,

123
00:09:18,492 --> 00:09:23,725
remote-tracking refs, fetch, pull and push.
I pass at 90 overall, with at least 70

124
00:09:23,765 --> 00:09:28,998
percent in each of the four parts. In the
hands-on part, the end state, the safety

125
00:09:29,038 --> 00:09:32,470
of the path and the explanation
are scored separately.

126
00:09:32,510 --> 00:09:37,743
I place every ref in its repository before
I reason about it, and I ask the server

127
00:09:37,783 --> 00:09:41,022
directly instead of trusting
a remote-tracking ref.

128
00:09:41,062 --> 00:09:45,523
After a miss I get the remediation
map and variant B, not the answers.

129
00:09:47,363 --> 00:09:52,300
Before the gate: answer the "Interview
questions" sections of Chapters 7, 8 and 12

130
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:52,664
aloud.

131
00:09:53,004 --> 00:09:56,856
You now know what Gate 2 asks,
and the habit it tests hardest.

132
00:09:56,896 --> 00:10:01,187
Practise before you sit it. Next
time: the undo map, and git restore.

133
00:10:01,227 --> 00:10:04,451
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

134
00:10:04,491 --> 00:10:05,957
See you in the next one.
